      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/glossolepis-incisus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The red rainbow fish, Glossolepis incisus, is one of the magnificent classics among the rainbow fish. This fish caused real storms of enthusiasm in the 1970s, when only two relatively inconspicuous species of this group of fish were known in the hobby.</p><p>Males reach 15 cm in length, while females remain smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/glossolepis-incisus-2/",
      "html": "<p>In the hobby, only bred ones are represented. Regarding the coloration, the red glittering splendour increases with every centimeter that the fish grows. But also the usually traded, 6-7 cm long animals are already very nicely colored.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/glossolepis-incisus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The home of Glossolepis incisus lies in western Papua New Guinea, where it inhabits Lake Sentani and its surroundings. Due to the increasing pollution of the lake, the species is considered endangered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/glossolepis-incisus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Males become 15 cm long, females remain smaller.</p>"
    }
